coder › Done. Here's what I changed and why.

## What changed

1. **New module `src/tokenize.ts`** — extracted `tokenize(text: string): string[]` using exactly `wordCount`'s behaviour: lower-case, a word is a run of letters/digits/apostrophes (`/[^a-z0-9']+/` as separator), and no empty words.

2. **`src/stats.ts`** — `wordCount` now returns `tokenize(text).length` (behaviour unchanged), and `topWords` builds its `Map` from `tokenize(text)` instead of its own `\s+` split + regex clean. This fixes the drift: it now counts case-insensitively and treats apostrophes/separators consistently. I also added alphabetical tie-breaking to `topWords`, and a comparison `(b[1] - a[1]) || alphabetical` so ties are sorted by word rather than left in insertion order.

3. **Tests** — added `src/tokenize.test.ts` (lower-casing, word characters, separators, no empty words) and extended `src/stats.test.ts` with `topWords` cases for case-insensitivity, top-n ordering, and alphabetical ties.

## One decision the plan implied but didn't spell out

The plan asked for "ties broken alphabetically", but the original `topWords` only sorted by count and returned ties in insertion order. I implemented the alphabetical tie-break explicitly rather than relying on `Map` preservation.

